  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Overview
    
    A deep-carved sanctuary beneath ☽'scar Ramtatus~, the primary Nightshelf colony settlement, dating to the Great Thaw. Its formal name appears in administrative ice from that era; no living Nightshelf penguin uses it. The site is called **the Scar**, and the name has outlasted every faction that tried to hold it. The sanctuary has passed through the control of criminal syndicates, endured cult desecration by the Wake-Singers — an orca shaman cult devoted to waking their dead god through blood sacrifice — and survived forced cleansing before its current tenure under ☽'ofund' Guild, a Nightshelf trade guild that operates it as a waystation and depot. The fire-core mine abutting the Marker's foundations is known among tunnel-runners as *the Scar-mouth*.
    
    ## Physical Stratigraphy
    
    The entrance tunnel descends through layered ice that constitutes its own historical record. The uppermost stratum is pale blue — original Nightfall Shelf settler ice. Below it, darker bands bear the carved symbols of the Midnight Claws, the syndicate that once controlled Nightshelf's shadow economy; most symbols were chiseled away after the Claws' dissolution, leaving gouges like claw-marks, though a few on the eastern wall still glow faintly and are reported to be thinning. Deeper still, glassy sections mark where Veil-render Mairae~, a Nightshelf cleansing adept, burned corruption back from the Marker's foundations with aurora-fire, leaving the stone rippled and brittle.
    
    The main chamber's western wall bears an unofficial memorial — names scratched into stone, some cuts deep enough to represent hours of work. These record settlers killed in the Faction Wars and workers lost when the Wake-Singers performed the rite known as Tide Fall Blasphemy, which shook the cavern hard enough to crack the ceiling. The repairs are conspicuously poor: mismatched ice-blocks set against original stone.
    
    ## The Marker and Ongoing Corruption
    
    Mairae~'s cleansing drove corruption from the Marker's foundations into deeper sealed passages rather than destroying it. The consequences are measurable. Goods stored below the second chamber absorb ice-memory during the Long Dark — fire-cores humming with dead miners' final shifts, aurora-crystals producing visions when handled bare — requiring ongoing sanctification maintenance. Fire-braziers extinguish without cause. The walls hum in deep chambers. The gouged stone retains warmth independent of any heat source.
    
    Miners working fire-core veins near the Marker report depths where the cold changes character: not numbing, but an airless stillness that leaves the body functional and the mind exposed. Aurora-ink inscriptions on artifacts stored in proximity to the Marker have burned black. One documented case involved a genealogical tome whose last three entries turned opaque, as though examined and marked by something within the sealed passages.
    
    ## The ☽'somunis' Mist~Record
    
    A shadow-forge vault beneath the sanctuary housed the ☽'somunis' Mist~Record — a fire-core tome of pre-penguin records bound in volcanic glass and bone — under a lineage of keepers whose fates are documented in the chronicle "Three Days" and the folk-song "The Scar-Mouth Song." The vault's fire-cores were changed on schedule. The tome was wrapped in bioluminescent cloth that was never replaced, because replacing it would mean touching the book. During the Clever Ice Age, someone attempted to burn the tome inside the vault, leaving a frost-crack through the back cover. The remnants of the ☽'shadow Covens' Order, the Nightshelf occult faction that administered the vault, would not clarify who was responsible.
    
    ## Current Status
    
    ☽'ofund' Guild maintains the site as a trade-post and secure storage depot, advertising fire-cores, aurora-crystals, and sealed vault space. No one lingers after the aurora dims. The tunnel-runners' saying persists: *the Scar remembers better than any of us, and it is still angry about what it held.*
